# Binaries must never get end-of-line conversion applied — that would corrupt
# them. TaskNook.exe is committed on purpose, so this matters here.
*.exe binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.db binary
*.otf binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Line endings are NOT cosmetic for these two launchers — they decide whether
# the file runs at all, so pin them instead of trusting each clone's
# core.autocrlf.
#
# build-exe.bat was committed LF-only and cmd.exe cannot parse that: it eats
# the first characters of every line (`REM` -> `M`, `echo` -> `cho`) and the
# build dies immediately. It survived only on GitHub's Windows runners, which
# default core.autocrlf=true and convert on checkout — so the build was broken
# on any machine that didn't, while CI stayed green.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
# The mirror image: TaskNook.command is run by a macOS/Linux shell, which
# treats a trailing CR as part of the command and fails with '\r: command not
# found'. It must stay LF even on a Windows checkout.
*.command text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
